'''wood<sup>2</sup>†''' (wöd), ''a.'' [Sc. ''wod, wud''; < ME. ''wood, woode, wod, wode'', < AS. ''wōd'', mad, raging, furious, = Icel. ''ōdhr'', raging, frantic, = Goth. ''wōds'', mad; cf. MD. ''woed, woede'', D. ''woede'', OHG. ''wuot'', MHG. G. ''wut, wuth'', madness; AS. ''wōd'', voice, song, = Icel. ''ōdhr'', song, poetry, mind, wit; prob. allied to L. ''vātes'', a prophet, bard (one filled with “a fine frenzy”): see ''vatic''. See ''Woden, Wednesday''.] Mad; frantic; furious; angry; enraged; raging. [Obsolete or prov. Eng. or Scotch.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Ffuerse Ector was fayn of his fyn helpe,
And as ''wode'' as a wild bore wan on his horse.</poem>{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 6523.}}<poem>Now a Monday next, at quarter nyght,
Shal falle a reyn, and that so wilde and ''wood''
That half so greet was nevere Noees flood.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 331.}}
Howard was as ''wode'' as a wilde bullok; God sende hym seche wurshipp as he deservith. ''Paston Letters'', I. 341.

Quyriache [Iscariot] sayd, Thou ''wood'' hounde [mad dog, margin] thou hist doon to me grete prouffyte [profit].{{right|''Ashton’s Legendary Hist. of the Cross'' (reprinted from orig. [ed. of Nov. 20, 1483), London, 1887, p. xxxvi.}}
Franticke companion, lunaticke and ''wood''.{{right|''Greene'', Orlando Furioso, l. 984.}}}}

'''For wood†''', like anything mad; “like mad.”
{{smaller block|<poem>Yit lat us to the peple seme . . .
That wimmen loves us ''for wood''.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 1747.}}}}

'''wood<sup>2</sup>†''' (wöd), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''wooden, wodien''; from the adj. Cf. ''weed''<sup>3</sup>.] '''1.''' To act like a madman; rave.
